/// The read tool prefixes every content line with its 1-based file
/// line number (`"123: "`). Split that prefix off so the highlighter
/// only sees the file content. Lines without a digit-colon prefix
/// (annotation lines like "(5 lines omitted)") come back unsplit.
fn split_line_number(line: &str) -> (Option<&str>, &str) {
    match line.find(':') {
        Some(pos) if pos > 0 && line[..pos].bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) => {
            let rest = line[pos + 1..].strip_prefix(' ').unwrap_or(&line[pos + 1..]);
            (Some(&line[..pos]), rest)
        }
        _ => (None, line),
    }
}
```

Note: byte-safe? `line[..pos]` where pos is a byte index from `find(':')` — fine, ':' is ASCII so it's on a char boundary. `line[pos+1..]` — also a boundary. `strip_prefix(' ')` on &str — takes char ✓.

Edge case: "5: " → find(':') = 1; "5" is all digits ✓; rest = line[2..] = " " → strip_prefix(' ') → "". ✓
Edge case: "12:34" → pos 2, "12" is digits, rest = "34" (no space, strip_prefix fails → "34"). This would mis-split a content line "12:34" — but in read output, such a line would appear as "N: 12:34", where N is the actual line number, and the first ':' is the real separator ✓. A raw content line without prefix "12:34" would only occur if... read_body's lines always have the "N: " prefix from the read tool (or are annotation lines without colons). Annotation lines: "(5 lines omitted)" — no colon → no split ✓. "(End of file - total 0 lines)" — no colon ✓. " ... (line truncated to N chars)" — no colon, starts with space — wait, this is part of a content line: "N: <truncated> ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)". First ':' is after N → split correctly ✓.

