But what if the user has already typed some text AFTER the `@query`? Like `@sr check this` — then cursor is after "this" (position 11). `at_token_info()` would find `@` at position 0, but the word boundary check would fail because 's' (a word char) follows the `@`... wait no.

Actually `at_token_info` finds the LAST `@` before the cursor that's at a word boundary. Let me re-read:

```rust
let at_pos = chars[..col].iter().rposition(|&c| c == '@')?;
if at_pos > 0 {
    let prev = chars[at_pos - 1];
    if is_word_char(prev) {
        return None;
    }
}
let query: String = chars[at_pos + 1..col].iter().collect();
```

So it finds the LAST `@` before the cursor, checks word boundary, and the query is everything between `@` and the cursor. If the draft is `@sr check this file`, the `@` is at position 0, prev is start-of-line (OK), and query would be `sr check this file` (chars from position 1 to cursor position 17). That's the entire rest of the line.

Hmm, that means `replace_at_token` would replace from position 0 to the cursor (position 17) with just `sr check this file`, giving `sr check this file`. That actually works correctly — it strips the `@` from the beginning and keeps everything else.

But wait — what if the `@` is in the middle of a multi-line draft? `at_token_info` only looks at the current line, so it only affects the current line. That's correct.

OK, I'm now confident the implementation is correct. Let me verify the build passes and all tests pass.

